.TH CHMAP_DESTROY_WITH_DTOR 3 "2026-08-19" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_destroy_with_dtor \- destroy a hash map, running a destructor on every value first, without allocating memory
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chashmap.h>

void chmap_destroy_with_dtor(chmap chm,
    void (*val_dtor)(cmap_pair *val_pair, void *dtor_ctx),
    void *dtor_ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Behaves like __chmap_destroy(3), except that val_dtor (when non-NULL) is
invoked once per live entry, passing that entry's own value as a
cmap_pair, immediately before the entry itself is freed.

Unlike enumerating the map with chashmap_begin_iter(3) first and
destroying it afterward, this function never allocates memory of its
own: it walks the map's own internal storage directly, the same way
__chmap_destroy(3) already does internally, with the destructor call
threaded into that same walk.

Intended for a map whose values are themselves owned pointers to a
larger structure needing its own recursive teardown (for example, a
dictionary of dictionaries). Destroying such a map by enumerating it
via chashmap_begin_iter(3), running a value destructor per entry, and
only then destroying the (by then already emptied) map has a real gap
under memory pressure: chashmap_begin_iter(3)'s own small internal
allocation can itself fail, in which case that approach has no way
left to reach and free the values it still owns. This function has no
such gap, since it performs no allocation of its own.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to destroy
val_dtor   Destructor invoked once per live value before that entry is
           freed, or NULL to behave identically to __chmap_destroy(3)
dtor_ctx   Opaque pointer forwarded unchanged to every val_dtor call
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call with a NULL chm (a no-op)
Does not NULL the chm handle, matching __chmap_destroy(3)'s own raw,
internal convention
val_dtor must not itself mutate chm; the map is mid-teardown for the
whole duration of this call
Performs no allocation of its own, so it cannot fail under any memory
condition that would make chashmap_begin_iter(3) itself fail
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR __chmap_destroy (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3)
